1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:12,666
The idea is old. In 1950, Turing describes learning as a path to machine intelligence.

2
00:00:12,666 --> 00:00:21,066
In 1957 Rosenblatt proposes the perceptron. In the same years, L M S appears in adaptive signal processing.

3
00:00:21,066 --> 00:00:27,933
In 1969, Minsky and Papert publish a book on the fundamental limitations of neural networks.

4
00:00:27,933 --> 00:00:35,966
In 1986, backpropagation becomes a practical way to train deep networks, and in 1989 LeNet reads handwriting.

5
00:00:35,966 --> 00:00:42,600
Then attention shifted to probabilistic methods and convex optimization, mostly on shallow models.

6
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:50,900
Around 2006 deep networks regain attention; in 2012 Krizhevsky's AlexNet beats all methods on ImageNet.

7
00:00:50,900 --> 00:01:00,466
From the perceptron to AlexNet is 55 years. The idea of a learned circuit is old; the breakthrough is recent.

8
00:01:00,466 --> 00:01:08,966
What limit was 1969 about? Take four points labeled exclusive or: plus one when exactly one input is on.

9
00:01:08,966 --> 00:01:16,700
One element draws one line. Try every line: some get three points right, none gets all four.

10
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:24,366
Stack two ramps on x1 plus x2, the second bending at 1, and read out first minus twice the second.

11
00:01:24,366 --> 00:01:33,833
Compute the four rows: y comes out 0, 0, 1, 1. That is exclusive or, exactly, with no line in sight.

12
00:01:33,833 --> 00:01:42,200
One element cannot, a stack can. Making stacks trainable is what backpropagation did in 1986.

13
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:49,200
Is deep learning engineering, or alchemy? The lecture puts that question next to a famous xkcd comic.

14
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:56,766
The comic's machine learning system: pour the data into a big pile of linear algebra, collect the answers on the other side.

15
00:01:56,766 --> 00:02:04,766
What if the answers are wrong? Just stir the pile until they start looking right. That is the alchemist's method.

16
00:02:04,766 --> 00:02:10,566
The learning rate decides whether training works. Try a bowl, starting from w equals 1.

17
00:02:10,566 --> 00:02:19,533
Try four learning rates. 0.1 and 0.4 settle to zero, 0.5 bounces forever, and 0.6 blows up.

18
00:02:19,533 --> 00:02:30,666
No magic: each step multiplies w by one minus four eta. It shrinks only while eta stays below two over four, which is 0.5.

19
00:02:30,666 --> 00:02:37,800
That is engineering: a knob with a reason, which predicts where it works and where it fails.

20
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:42,200
Three earlier courses feed this one, and they line up with the definition.

21
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:49,766
Machine learning brings the patterns, optimization the circuits and knobs, probability the new data.

22
00:02:49,766 --> 00:02:57,300
So the course is about designing, visualizing and understanding deep networks, and knowing why they work.

23
00:02:57,300 --> 00:03:02,300
A companion reading: Terence Tao's essay, Mathematics in the Age of AI.

24
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:11,500
It sets aside what AI tools can do and asks what the goals and values of a field are. We can ask the same of deep learning.

